{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-122292",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the event that two heads are obtained, and let $B$ be the event that a fair coin is chosen.\n\nTo calculate the probability of getting two heads, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(A|B)P(B)+P(A|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since among $100$ coins there is one unfair coin, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=\\frac{99}{100},\\,P({B}^{′})=\\frac{1}{100}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of getting two heads if the unfair coin is chosen is $1,$ and the probability of getting two heads if the fair coin is chosen is $\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A|B)=\\frac{1}{4},\\,P(A|{B}^{′})=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability of getting  two heads is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|B)P(B)+P(A|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{99}{100})+(1)(\\frac{1}{100}) \\\\  & =\\frac{99}{400}+\\frac{1}{100} \\\\  & =\\frac{103}{400}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
